krw wrote:
> In article <wct7l.113269$La5.76662@fe03.news.easynews.com>, 
> phil.hates.spam@amadeus.munged.eclipse.co.uk says...> 
>> <snip>
>> FWIW, daisy printers were capable of half-spacing (and less), which gave 
>> a sort of "bold" effect, but the solenoids didn't need to retract  -- 
> 
> Never took apart a daisy wheel printer, so just asking...  Wouldn't 
> that give a smeared effect?  The "bold" would only be one-
> dimensional.  Why wouldn't the solenoids need to retract?  They 
> must have to somehow.  Also, half-spacing (or 1/10th or whatever 
> for bold) would need to have carriage/wheel stops every micro-
> space, right?  Seems like an expensive and non optimal design.
> 
>> IIRC, the printer hammer was actuated by the solenoid, but then flew 
>> ballistically, to strike an appropriately positioned daisy petal
> 
> A ballistic hammer would only add to the complexity, hammer delay, 
> and mass.  Seems wasteful.

actually, it wasn't smeared -- although the use of one-time plastic 
ribbon helped, as opposed to ink-soaked fabric

I don't know if the printhead was really continuously driven, or simply 
appeared to move continuously under the control of a stepper motor, but 
I would guess it was the latter

the wheel needs to stop, certainly, but there's actually no need to stop 
the whole printhead  --  it's just a case of timing the strike so that 
the hammer will arrive where the printhead is going to be in a few 
milliseconds time

I do remember that the engineers could adjust it so that all the print 
columns were vertically aligned, instead of drifting to the left or the 
right -- they did this on delivery, but it was rarely necessary after that

my recollection is that half-spacing just looked ugly, overstriking at 
1/16 of the character width would thicken the verticals, but do nothing 
for the horizontals (except fill in the gaps), which looked bolder, but 
one-dimensional, as you said, while a few more sixteenths would give a 
double-struck effect (i.e, two verticals would have white space between, 
instead of overlapping) which was useful for titles (provided you'd 
double-spaced the T E X T )

I think you could program it to strike twice in the same space, without 
advancing between strikes (the ribbon advanced, the printhead did not), 
which looked blacker, but only with fabric ribbons

it troubles me that I cannot remember how the hammer was returned -- the 
petal would return to its upright position, but was it still in contact 
with the hammer at this point? surely not?  the petals were prone to 
fracture, so you'd end up with a basketful of daisies, all lacking the 
letter 'e', which doesn't sound like a reliable source of energy for 
returning the hammer

for its time, I thought it was wonderful   . . .   /phil


