Having a single-pass plastic ribbon instead of a multipass inked fabric one 
certainly helped, but it wasn't necessary for some moving-typeslug designs. 
Look at output from a properly-adjusted 1443 (yes, there occasionally *were* 
correctly-adjusted 1443s): even without the noticable (but not 
objectionable) smearing of a 1403N1 to compare it to, the 1443 with its 
continuously-moving shuttlebar produced lovely sharp glpyhs.


> I don't know if the printhead was really continuously driven, or simply 
> appeared to move continuously under the control of a stepper motor, but I 
> would guess it was the latter
>
> the wheel needs to stop, certainly, but there's actually no need to stop 
> the whole printhead  --  it's just a case of timing the strike so that the 
> hammer will arrive where the printhead is going to be in a few 
> milliseconds time

...with the usual consequences of a mistimed hammer fire which resulted in 
impact between two daisywheel tines, or impact while the wheel is still in 
motion.  Different architecture but same failure concept as what produced 
chipped-tooth golfballs in a Selectric.  Somewhere I've got examples of both 
daisywheels and golfballs that were munged by the printer.

Joe Morris 




