In article <MPG.23c81ed76b0a7513989792@news.individual.net>,
krw@att.zzzzzzzzz (krw) writes:

> In article <wct7l.113269$La5.76662@fe03.news.easynews.com>,
> phil.hates.spam@amadeus.munged.eclipse.co.uk says...>
>
>> FWIW, daisy printers were capable of half-spacing (and less),
>> which gave a sort of "bold" effect, but the solenoids didn't
>> need to retract  --
>
> Never took apart a daisy wheel printer, so just asking...  Wouldn't
> that give a smeared effect?  The "bold" would only be one-
> dimensional.  Why wouldn't the solenoids need to retract?  They
> must have to somehow.  Also, half-spacing (or 1/10th or whatever
> for bold) would need to have carriage/wheel stops every micro-
> space, right?  Seems like an expensive and non optimal design.

Yup.  On the other tentacle, one PPOE was enamored by bold headings,
even on a good old line printer.  Hence we had to write all programs
to print each heading and total line twice.  And since the printer had
an oscillating typebar, we had to "print" a blank line between the two
impressions - otherwise it would look _really_ smeared.

Ironically, with a fresh ribbon it didn't really make that much
difference.  And, as I demonstrated by omitting the overprinting
from a program, reports - especially ones with lots of total lines -
printed _much_ faster.  But - in a classic case of double ironies
canceling eath other out - the overprinting became more effective
the more worn the printer ribbon was.  And we never replaced a ribbon
until every last bit of ink had been wrung out of it.  Unless we were
printing cheques in a payroll or accounts receivable run, which were
considered worthy of a ribbon whose newness was in direct proportion
to the size of the client.

>> IIRC, the printer hammer was actuated by the solenoid, but then flew
>> ballistically, to strike an appropriately positioned daisy petal
>
> A ballistic hammer would only add to the complexity, hammer delay,
> and mass.  Seems wasteful.

But not unprecedented - IIRC the model 35 Teletype used a ballistic
hammer.

