phil chastney wrote:
> 
> as shown in the earlier link to VT100 pages, the DECWriter had no
> descenders, so that, e.g, lowercase 'g' was raised so that its tail sat
> on the baseline  --  as you say, horrendously unattractive

AFAIR, that was true of all 7- or 8-pin dot-matrix printers (including
for example many Epsons) when running in draft mode; putting two pins
below the baseline wouldn't have left sufficient resolution for
well-formed characters.

At least some of those printers, though, could render text with
descenders in "NLQ" ("near-letter-quality") mode, by overprinting and
microfeeding. Everything above the baseline was printed on one pass,
the paper was advanced less than the line-height, and the descenders
were printed in a second pass. This meant driving the printer in
graphics mode rather than text mode, of course, and rendering the
characters in software. I recall a DOS utility named "lq.exe" that
provided NLQ mode for some Epsons, with a variety of fonts (eg Palatino).

The 9-pin dot-matrix printers, such as the Epson FX-80, could print
proper descenders in draft and double-strike modes. (Double-strike was
the printer's built-in high-quality print mode, darker and more
readable than draft print. There was a separate "emphasized" setting
that produced boldface text.)

The FX-80 actually had a nice array of features for a home printer of
its era (mid-1980s). Its built-in text modes included pica (10 cpi)
and elite (12 cpi) pitches, monospace or proportional printing,
italics, underline, super/subscript, etc. Graphics modes included
"quad-density" (240 dpi), the ability to address all 9 pins, and so
on. It supported half-speed printing (for noise reduction),
character-at-a-time (for TTY-style use), downloaded fonts, and a bunch
of other nifty tricks. With high-quality microperforated fan-fold
paper, it could be used to create decent black & white output.

I used my FX-80 right up until I got my first laser printer.

-- 
Michael Wojcik
Micro Focus
Rhetoric & Writing, Michigan State University


